INT. IRON TEMPLE GYM - NIGHT

Empty. Dim emergency lights. Rows of dead treadmills. A neon sign above the juice bar flickers: NO PAIN NO GAIN.

A door SLAMS somewhere deep inside the building. Footsteps. Then a figure comes through the locker room entrance in a black hoodie and gym bag, moving fast.

Mo, 30s, wiry, nervous hands. He drops the bag onto a bench and unzips it. Inside: a stethoscope, a tablet, a roll of thermal paper, an old key on a lanyard.

He taps the tablet. A blue glow lights his face. A text appears on screen:

ON TABLET: "Kip's on the door. Kim's got the cameras. You've got 4 minutes."

MO
(to himself)
Kip. Kim. Kip. Kim. Why do they both sound the same.

He pockets the key. Crosses to the front desk, where a night janitor in headphones mops the floor, oblivious, humming. Mo ducks behind a rack of kettlebells. The janitor sways closer, closer, then turns away.

MO (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Good. Go. Go home.

The janitor stops. Takes off one earphone. Mo freezes. A phone BUZZES somewhere. The janitor answers it, laughs, wanders toward the back office, still talking.

Mo exhales. Moves.

At the back of the gym he finds the glass trophy case. Inside: a small gold dumbbell, "1987 Regional Powerlifting Champion." He reads the plaque and laughs once.

MO (CONT'D)
Big Dave's dumbbell. Unbelievable.

He takes out the old key. Slides it into the lock. It does not turn. He tries again. Nothing.

MO (CONT'D)
Come on come on come on

His thumb catches. The lock CLICKS. The glass door swings open.

He lifts the dumbbell carefully. It's heavier than it looks. He weighs it in his hand, looking at it the way a man looks at the thing that is about to change his life.

The tablet BUZZES again. He glances.

ON TABLET: "Kip says there's a guy outside. Big. Beard. Holding a coffee."

MO (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
Big Dave.

He shoves the dumbbell into the bag. Zips it. Looks around for an exit. The front is out. The side door is alarmed. The only way is through the pool area, and past the janitor, who has begun to sing along to whatever is in his headphones.

Mo crouches. Creeps along the wall of treadmills. A treadmill SCREAMS to life as he brushes the start button, belt spinning, display lights flashing. He slaps the stop button. It keeps going.

MO (CONT'D)
No no no no.

The janitor stops singing. Footsteps approach from the back office. Mo dives behind the juice bar, hugging the bag. The janitor appears, scratches his head at the roaring treadmill, and hits the emergency stop with the flat of his hand. Silence.

The janitor mutters something in Spanish, puts his earphone back in, and goes back to mopping.

Mo breathes. Counts to ten. Then slides out from behind the bar and makes for the pool doors, the bag bumping his hip.

At the pool doors he hesitates. Through the glass: a figure in the water, swimming slow laps in the dark. A woman, silver cap, goggles. She doesn't look up. Mo slips past on the tile, one hand on the wall.

He reaches the fire door. Presses the bar. It opens without a sound.

The alarm does not go off.

MO (CONT'D)
(realising)
Kim.

He steps out into the cold. The door CLOSES softly behind him.

EXT. GYM PARKING LOT - CONTINUOUS

A blue van idles. Its back door slides open. Inside, nothing but darkness and the red glow of a cigarette.

Mo throws the bag in. Climbs after.

MO
Go.

The van pulls away. In the window of the gym, the neon sign flickers once and dies.
